                <pb facs="0319" n="247" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg247"/>

                <head>No. 14.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Earl Granville to Mr. Lowell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-09-02"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 2,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of yous letter of the 1st instant, urging the release from prison, on the ground
                    of ill-health, of Mr. Joseph B. Walsh, who was arrested at Castlebar, in the
                    month of March last, under the protection of persons and property (Ireland) act,
                    1881.</p>
                <p>In reply, I beg leave to state to you that I have lost no time in referring your
                    letter to the proper department of Her Majesty’s Government, and that I shall
                    not fail to communicate further with you upon this subject.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GRANVILLE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
